    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d113">

                <pb facs="0198" n="118" sameAs="frus:frus1936v01/pg118"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">500.A15A5/845: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in the United Kingdom</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Bingham</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">London</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1936-12-14T17:00:00+00:00">December 14,
                            1936—5 p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received December 14—12:45 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>609. Department’s 447, December 12, 3 p.m. Foreign Office confirmed again today
                    its understanding that the French Government desires to be in a position to
                    ratify if and when negotiations with Germany are concluded. However, both the
                    French and British Governments are desirous of avoiding a lapse.</p>
                <p>Ciano has informed Drummond it would be difficult for the Italian Government to
                    sign the treaty until it has been ratified by the original signatories.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Bingham</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
